      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Exercise: Reinhard color transfer (wear another photo's palette)\n\nReinhard's method recolors a SOURCE image to carry a TARGET image's palette using nothing but per-channel statistics. Work in CIELab (L = lightness, a = green-red, b = blue-yellow): its axes are nearly independent, so shifting each on its own does not skew the others. For every channel: subtract the source mean, rescale by (target std / source std), add the target mean - the result then carries the target's average color AND its spread. 'CV Array Statistic' emits one mean (and one std) per channel as a 1-D NPARRAY that broadcasts straight into cv2.subtract / multiply / divide / add, exactly like a 'CV Scalar'. The arithmetic runs on a float32 copy of the Lab image (the signed, rescaled values would clip in uint8); 'CV Cast Array' back to uint8 saturates into range before Lab -> BGR.\n\nCONTRAST (lower branch): matching only the MEAN (subtract source mean, add target mean, SKIP the std rescale) shifts the average color but keeps the source's own contrast, so a punchy target comes out flat and a flat target comes out over-punchy. The full transfer matches the spread too - compare the two result previews and the printed std readouts.\n\nPure composition, no bespoke node: cvtColor + 'CV Array Statistic' + generic arithmetic. Swap the two Load Image nodes to transfer any palette onto any photo. (A perfectly flat channel has std 0 and would divide 0/0; real photographs never do - feed real imagery, not a synthetic constant.)"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
